import Foundation

// MARK: - Leading Like Jesus Content Expansion (Entries 9–20)

extension ContentLibrary {

    static let leadershipContentExpansion: [DayContent] = [
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Proverbs 11:14",
            scriptureText: "For lack of guidance a nation falls, but victory is won through many advisers.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Humility to Seek Counsel",
            devotionalText: "The best leaders don't have all the answers — they surround themselves with wise advisers. Seeking counsel isn't a sign of weakness; it's a mark of wisdom. When you lead in isolation, you lead in danger. Today, consider who speaks into your leadership. Are you open to advice, or have you closed the door to other voices?",
            prayerText: "Lord, give me the humility to seek counsel. Surround me with wise advisers who love You and will speak truth into my life. Protect me from the arrogance of leading alone. Help me listen well and act wisely. Victory comes through many counselors, so give me ears to hear and a heart that's teachable. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Who are the advisers in your life, and when was the last time you genuinely sought their counsel?",
            actionSteps: ["Identify one decision you're facing and ask a trusted person for their honest input", "Thank someone who has given you wise counsel in the past"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Matthew 20:25-28",
            scriptureText: "Jesus called them together and said, 'You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their high officials exercise authority over them. Not so with you. Instead, whoever wants to become great among you must be your servant.'",
            devotionalTitle: "Leading Down, Not Up",
            devotionalText: "The world's model of leadership is authority over people. Jesus' model is service under people. He didn't climb ladders — He washed feet. Leading like Jesus means your position exists to serve others, not to elevate yourself. The higher your influence, the lower your posture should be. Today, lead by serving someone you have authority over.",
            prayerText: "Jesus, Your leadership was service. Help me lead like You — not by lording authority over others, but by serving them. Transform my understanding of leadership. Let my position be a platform for blessing, not power. Help me lead down, not up. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "In your leadership, are you more focused on your authority or your service?",
            actionSteps: ["Do one task today that's 'beneath' your position as an act of servant leadership", "Ask someone you lead: 'How can I serve you better?'"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Joshua 1:9",
            scriptureText: "Have I not commanded you? Be strong and courageous. Do not be afraid; do not be discouraged, for the Lord your God will be with you wherever you go.",
            devotionalTitle: "Courage for the Called",
            devotionalText: "God's command to Joshua came at a terrifying moment — stepping into Moses' shoes to lead an entire nation. The remedy for leadership fear isn't self-confidence; it's God-confidence. He doesn't say 'be strong because you're capable.' He says 'be strong because I'm with you.' Every leader faces moments of fear. The question isn't whether you'll be afraid, but whether you'll trust God through it.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I need courage for the leadership You've given me. I'm sometimes afraid and often discouraged. But I hear Your command: be strong and courageous, for You are with me. I don't lead alone. Give me God-confidence that overcomes my insecurity. Wherever I go, You go with me. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What aspect of your leadership role makes you most afraid or discouraged?",
            actionSteps: ["Face one leadership challenge today with the confidence that God is with you", "Write Joshua 1:9 where you'll see it during your leadership responsibilities"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "1 Timothy 4:12",
            scriptureText: "Don't let anyone look down on you because you are young, but set an example for the believers in speech, in conduct, in love, in faith and in purity.",
            devotionalTitle: "Leading by Example",
            devotionalText: "Paul told Timothy that the most powerful form of leadership isn't a title — it's an example. People follow what they see, not just what they hear. Your speech, conduct, love, faith, and purity are all forms of leadership. Whether or not you have a formal role, your life is leading someone. The question is: where are you leading them?",
            prayerText: "Lord, make my life an example worth following. Purify my speech, my conduct, my love, my faith. I know people are watching — help me lead them toward You by the way I live. I don't need a title to lead. I need a life that reflects Your character. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "If someone followed your example for a week, where would they end up?",
            actionSteps: ["Choose one area — speech, conduct, love, faith, or purity — and focus on exemplifying it today", "Ask someone you trust for honest feedback on how your life models faith"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Nehemiah 2:17-18",
            scriptureText: "Then I said to them, 'You see the trouble we are in: Jerusalem lies in ruins, and its gates have been burned with fire. Come, let us rebuild the wall of Jerusalem, and we will no longer be in disgrace.'",
            devotionalTitle: "Casting Vision in the Rubble",
            devotionalText: "Nehemiah stood in literal rubble and cast a vision for rebuilding. Great leaders don't ignore problems — they name them honestly and then paint a picture of what could be. 'Come, let us rebuild' is one of the most powerful leadership phrases in Scripture. It acknowledges the mess, invites participation, and points toward a future worth building. Lead like Nehemiah today.",
            prayerText: "Lord, give me the courage to name the rubble in my world and the faith to cast vision for rebuilding. Help me not just see problems but imagine solutions. Give me words that inspire others to join the work. Like Nehemiah, help me say 'come, let us rebuild' with conviction and hope. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Where do you see rubble that needs rebuilding, and what vision could you cast for restoration?",
            actionSteps: ["Identify one broken area in your sphere of influence and write a vision for what it could become", "Share that vision with one person and invite them to join you"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Philippians 2:3-4",
            scriptureText: "Do nothing out of selfish ambition or vain conceit. Rather, in humility value others above yourselves, not looking to your own interests but each of you to the interests of the others.",
            devotionalTitle: "Leading Without Selfish Ambition",
            devotionalText: "Selfish ambition is the toxic twin of godly leadership. It looks like leadership from the outside but serves only the leader. Paul draws a clear line: value others above yourself. Look to their interests, not just your own. This doesn't mean ignoring your needs, but making sure your leadership is genuinely for the benefit of those you lead, not just for your advancement.",
            prayerText: "Lord, root out any selfish ambition in my leadership. I want to lead for others, not for myself. Give me the humility to value the people I lead above my own interests. Help me check my motives regularly. Let my leadership be marked by selflessness, not self-promotion. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Honestly, is your leadership more about serving others or advancing yourself?",
            actionSteps: ["Before your next leadership decision, ask: 'Who does this benefit most — me or them?'", "Publicly celebrate someone else's win instead of your own today"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Exodus 18:17-18",
            scriptureText: "Moses' father-in-law replied, 'What you are doing is not good. You and these people who come to you will only wear yourselves out. The work is too heavy for you; you cannot handle it alone.'",
            devotionalTitle: "You Can't Do It All",
            devotionalText: "Even Moses — chosen by God, parting seas, receiving commandments — needed someone to tell him he was doing too much. Jethro's advice was revolutionary: delegate. The best leaders don't do everything; they empower others to share the load. If you're exhausted from leading, the problem might not be the workload — it might be that you haven't shared it.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I confess I try to do too much. Like Moses, I need to learn to delegate and trust others. Show me what only I can do and help me release the rest. Protect me from burnout by giving me the wisdom to share the work. Raise up capable people around me and help me trust them. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What are you doing that someone else could do if you empowered them?",
            actionSteps: ["Identify one responsibility you can delegate this week and hand it off", "Ask for help with one task you've been carrying alone"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Galatians 6:9",
            scriptureText: "Let us not become weary in doing good, for at the proper time we will reap a harvest if we do not give up.",
            devotionalTitle: "Leadership Fatigue",
            devotionalText: "Leadership is tiring. The results are slow, the criticism is loud, and the sacrifices are constant. Paul acknowledges this weariness but urges persistence. The harvest is coming — at the proper time, not your time. Don't give up because you can't see results yet. Keep doing good. Keep leading faithfully. The proper time is closer than you think.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I'm weary from leading. The results feel slow and the criticism feels heavy. Renew my strength and my vision. Help me not give up. I trust that a harvest is coming at the proper time. Give me endurance for today and hope for tomorrow. I will keep doing good because You are faithful. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Where are you most tempted to give up in your leadership, and what would help you persevere?",
            actionSteps: ["Take a 15-minute break today to rest and recharge — leadership fatigue is real", "Write down one reason you started leading and let it re-motivate you"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Proverbs 29:18",
            scriptureText: "Where there is no revelation, people cast off restraint; but blessed is the one who heeds wisdom's instruction.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Power of Vision",
            devotionalText: "Without vision, people drift. They lose focus, motivation, and direction. As a leader, one of your most important roles is to keep the vision alive and clear. This doesn't mean inventing your own vision — it means seeking God's revelation for the people and situations He's entrusted to you. Pray for vision, communicate it clearly, and watch people rally around God's purposes.",
            prayerText: "Lord, give me vision for the people and responsibilities You've entrusted to me. Don't let us drift for lack of direction. Reveal Your purposes and help me communicate them clearly. I want to lead people toward Your plans, not my own. Give me wisdom to guide well and vision to inspire. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Do the people you lead have a clear sense of where you're heading together?",
            actionSteps: ["Spend 10 minutes asking God for His vision for your leadership context", "Share a clear, compelling vision with the people you lead this week"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "John 10:11",
            scriptureText: "I am the good shepherd. The good shepherd lays down his life for the sheep.",
            devotionalTitle: "Leading Like a Shepherd",
            devotionalText: "Jesus' leadership metaphor of choice was a shepherd — not a CEO, not a general, not a king. A shepherd knows each sheep by name, leads them to nourishment, protects them from danger, and is willing to sacrifice everything for their safety. Your leadership should make people feel known, fed, protected, and valued. That's the shepherd's way.",
            prayerText: "Jesus, You are the Good Shepherd. Help me lead like You — knowing the people entrusted to me, feeding them what they need, protecting them from harm, and sacrificing for their good. I don't want to be a hireling who runs when things get hard. I want to be a shepherd who stays. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Do the people you lead feel known, fed, and protected by your leadership?",
            actionSteps: ["Learn something personal about someone you lead — their story, their struggles, their hopes", "Sacrifice something for the benefit of your team this week"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "James 3:1",
            scriptureText: "Not many of you should become teachers, my fellow believers, because you know that we who teach will be judged more strictly.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Weight of Influence",
            devotionalText: "Leadership carries weight. James warns that those who lead and teach will face a stricter standard. This isn't meant to scare you — it's meant to sober you. Your words, decisions, and example carry more impact than you realize. Treat your influence with the seriousness it deserves. Lead carefully, speak thoughtfully, and live with integrity.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I take the weight of leadership seriously. I know my influence carries responsibility. Help me lead with integrity — matching my private life with my public role. Guard my words, purify my motives, and keep me accountable. I want to lead in a way that honors the trust people have placed in me. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Are you treating your leadership influence with the seriousness it deserves?",
            actionSteps: ["Ask someone to hold you accountable in one specific area of your leadership", "Examine one leadership decision you made recently — was it worthy of the influence you carry?"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Isaiah 40:11",
            scriptureText: "He tends his flock like a shepherd: He gathers the lambs in his arms and carries them close to his heart; he gently leads those that have young.",
            devotionalTitle: "Gentle Leadership",
            devotionalText: "God's leadership style includes gathering, carrying, and gently leading. He pays special attention to the vulnerable — the lambs, those with young. The best leaders aren't the loudest or most forceful. They're the ones who notice who's struggling, who gathers the overlooked, who leads with gentleness. Today, lead gently. The people around you need a shepherd, not a drill sergeant.",
            prayerText: "Lord, teach me to lead gently. Help me gather those who are struggling, carry those who can't keep up, and lead with patience and tenderness. Show me who needs extra care today. I want to lead like You — close to my heart, with gentleness and strength. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Who in your sphere of influence needs a gentler touch from your leadership?",
            actionSteps: ["Check in on the most vulnerable person in your group and offer encouragement", "Lead one conversation this week with gentleness as your primary goal"]
        ),
    ]
}
